            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Social Issues and Culture</strong><br>\nHis government also focused on social issues, abolition of the death penalty, and the promotion of cultural projects, such as the Grand Louvre project and the construction of the Bibliothèque nationale de France.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Longevity</strong><br>\nMitterrand was re-elected in 1988, making him the longest-serving president of France at the time. His second term was marked by cohabitation with a conservative government and personal health challenges.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nMitterrand's presidency is marked by a legacy of social change, European integration, and political resilience. He died in 1996, a year after his presidency ended, leaving a complex legacy influenced by both his achievements and controversies.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
